text:
I already have a CA architecture running with 10,000 cells: the agents vote, generate PnL, change strategies, and continue trading nonstop. However, in practice, two operational bottlenecks have emerged that degrade the system's quality and require an engineering solution directly within the pipeline.
First, the input
: 50 features are fed in as a flat vector, where momentum, volatility, RSI, and the Hurst exponent are “unaware” of their interrelationships. As a result, a single indicator spike can easily turn into a false signal.
Second, topology
: a lattice with a Moore neighborhood propagates regime changes too slowly, and geometric neighbors are often semantically unrelated. At the same time, strict operational requirements remain in place: the implementation must run in MQL5, without matrices, without computationally intensive learning, and without slowing down OnTick().
In this article, we formalize the problem at the points where the system actually loses quality — in ExtractFeatures() and in the definition of neighbors and voting weights — and propose a practical upgrade that preserves compatibility with the existing Expert Advisor interface.
The market is more like a graph: momentum is linked to volatility, RSI is linked to the position within the Bollinger Bands, volume is linked to breakouts, autocorrelation is linked to the Hurst exponent, and the time of day changes the significance of the same patterns. If the features are related to one another in a nonlinear way, then the cellular automaton must evolve not only on a square grid, but on a graph of market relationships.
In this part, we enhance the architecture without external libraries, without matrices, and without computationally intensive learning. The idea is simple: to represent the input features as a graph, and the cells themselves as a graph of agents. In that case, the system operates as a distributed network. Each cell receives information not only from its geometric neighbors but also from semantic neighbors with the same feature or a similar strategy, as well as through long-range small-world connections.
Purpose of the article:
show why we should convert features into a graph;
describe graph-based filtering of 50 market features;
show how to replace some of the neighbors in a cellular automaton with semantic connections;
maintain compatibility with MQL5 and the old Expert Advisor interface;
provide practical criteria for determining when the graph-based version outperforms the standard CA lattice.
Why a Flat Feature Vector Loses Information
In the classic implementation, ExtractFeatures() returns an array:
double
feat[CNN_FEATURES];
// 50 features normalized to the range [-1..+1]
This is convenient as a data representation. From the perspective of market structure, however, it is a gross oversimplification. The array does not know that feat[0] is short-term momentum, feat[10] is short-term volatility, feat[16] is RSI14, feat[22] is the price’s position within the Bollinger Bands, and feat[36] is fractal time-series memory. For an array, these are simply adjacent or non-adjacent memory cells.
The problem arises when the model interprets a feature in isolation. For example, high momentum could mean:
trend continuation if volatility is moderate and the Hurst exponent is greater than 0.5;
a spurious spike if volatility is extreme and volume does not confirm the move;
a reversal zone if the price is near the upper Bollinger Band and the RSI is overbought;
a transitional regime if the autocorrelation changes sign.
In other words, a feature becomes informative only in the context of other features. Therefore, the second version of the architecture adds a layer of graph-based feature diffusion: each feature is not mixed with all others at once, but only with predefined semantic neighbors.
