This is not a neural network layer in the traditional sense. There are no trainable matrices here, no backpropagation, and no risk of causing computational complexity to skyrocket. This is a graph filter: it propagates information across the semantic topology of features and makes the input more resistant to noise from a single indicator.
Spectral Interpretation: The Market as a Signal on a Graph
If features are the nodes of a graph, then the entire market snapshot can be understood as a signal on the graph. The current state of the market is not a vector of numbers, but rather a distribution of energy across nodes. Momentum, volatility, volume, RSI, and the Hurst exponent together define the shape of the signal. The graph layer functions as a gentle spectral filter: it suppresses isolated spikes and enhances coherent structures.
Mathematically, this can be written as follows:
f' = tanh( α · f + β · A_local · f + γ · A_smallworld · f )
Where:
Symbol
Meaning
f
initial vector of 50 features
A_local
matrix of local connections between similar features
A_smallworld
matrix of long-range connections between different groups of features
α
weight of the feature itself
β
weight of the local context
γ
weight of long-range resonance
In MQL5, we do not create the matrix explicitly. This is important. We implement the same operator using neighbor indices to maintain performance and compatibility with the terminal.
