Such a graph brings the system closer to the real market: rather than an indicator voting on its own, a group of features forms a local probability field.
Now that we have learned how to filter the input signal using a feature graph, the next logical step is to apply the same philosophy to the topology of the cells themselves. If features are semantically related, then the cells that “read” these features must exchange information not only on a geometric basis but also on a semantic basis.
Cellular Automata Graph: From a Lattice to a Network of Agents
In the first version, each cell had eight Moore neighbors on a toroidal grid. This is elegant and fast, but geometrically arbitrary. Why should a cell with the RSI feature listen to the cell to its left if that cell happens to use a volume-based strategy? In the graph version, we retain some of the spatial connections but add semantic connections.
