Why Small-World Topology Is Important for the Market
A conventional lattice propagates information slowly. It takes many CA steps for a signal to travel from one corner of the grid to another. A small-world graph solves this problem: a few long-range connections drastically reduce the average path length between agents. For the trading system, this means that the new market regime spreads more quickly across the network.
In practical terms:
A volatility breakout affects trend-following cells more quickly;
The RSI signal reaches mean-reversion cells more quickly;
A successful strategy spreads its influence to other agents more quickly;
A faulty local cluster is not permanently isolated because it receives corrective connections from afar.
This is especially important for markets with abrupt transitions: news events, session openings, changes in liquidity, and the transition from a range-bound market during the Asian session to a trend during the European session.
