Result: CA Becomes the Market's Graph-based Nervous System
In the first part, the cellular automaton solved the main problem of fixed weights: it allowed the parameters to evolve directly during trading. In the second part, we removed the next limitation: the flat representation of features and the restriction of cells to a regular grid.
We converted the two key bottlenecks in the CA system into working graph-based structures, implementing the changes in practice rather than merely proposing them conceptually. What exactly has been implemented and where to apply the changes:
ApplyFeatureGraphLayer() — this lightweight graph diffusion step is called at the end of ExtractFeatures(), converting 50 numbers into a signal on the graph and suppressing isolated outliers;
BuildGraphNeighbors() — precomputation of neighbors: 4 local + 4 semantic (same feature group, next feature, same strategy, small-world chord);
GraphCoherence() and WeightedGraphVote() — voting with a bonus for coherence with the graph neighborhood;
GetGraphHealth() is a new diagnostic metric that complements Network Health and provides real-time decision thresholds.
Practical effects: fewer false signals from isolated indicator spikes; faster propagation of a new regime through small-world connections; formal criteria (Network Health + Graph Health) for increasing or decreasing confidence and managing risk. From an engineering standpoint, all of this is built into the engine (the OnInit/OnTick interface remains the same), without external libraries and without backpropagation.
The next step is to make the graph's connections adaptive: strengthen the edges when they generate profit together, and weaken them when they lead to errors. This will transform the current implementation into an evolutionary graph neural network within MetaTrader 5, while preserving the fundamental simplicity and measurability of the approach.
